@packages/compiler/src/event-protocol.sigil import { SigilCompilationEventProtocol }
@packages/compiler/src/report-protocol.sigil import { SigilCompilationReportProtocol }

component SigilCompilationEventReader {
  goal {
    Own bounded consumption and validation of one SigilCompilationEventProtocol
    byte stream without owning producer delivery or host transport cleanup.
  }

  interface {
    CompilationEventReaderProtocol {
      validateStream(readableSource, ExpectedCompilationRun, cancellationSignal)
      validates the expectation, consumes exactly one run, learns runId from started,
      and returns its validated completed, failed, or cancelled terminal,
      protocol-invalid, or source-unavailable. A successful terminal returns only
      after source end; every failure stops immediately without another read.

      ReadableEnvelopeSource asynchronously returns one non-empty raw byte chunk,
      end-of-source, or source-failed per read. The reader owns strict UTF-8 decoding
      and LF or CRLF framing across arbitrary chunk boundaries. Each non-empty frame
      contains exactly one JSON object and no trailing non-whitespace bytes.

      cancellationSignal is required and aborts a pending read. Validate the expected
      run first, then register cancellation before source access. An already-aborted
      signal returns source-unavailable without reading. For a pending read, the
      first cancellation or source settlement observed wins; once a source result is
      accepted, later cancellation does not replace processing of that chunk. Fully
      process the accepted result first. If it produces a terminal failure or
      terminal plus end-of-source, return that result; otherwise cancellation
      observed before the next read returns source-unavailable and no next read
      begins, including when a terminal is awaiting source closure.

      Before every return path, remove or permanently render inert the reader's
      cancellation registration and discard reader-owned framing state so a later
      signal cannot invoke settled validation work.

      Invalid ExpectedCompilationRun returns protocol-invalid before cancellation
      inspection, registration, or source access; cancellation state is irrelevant on
      that path.

      A generic source throw, rejection, empty chunk, undeclared result,
      source-failed, host capacity exhaustion, or end before a complete terminal
      frame returns source-unavailable and performs no further read. Invalid UTF-8,
      blank or malformed complete frames, or schema violations return
      protocol-invalid.

      On end-of-source, first finalize strict UTF-8 decoding. Invalid or incomplete
      UTF-8 returns protocol-invalid; otherwise an unterminated frame, missing
      started, or missing terminal returns source-unavailable.

      After a terminal frame, continue until end-of-source. Any additional non-empty
      byte, frame, or event is protocol-invalid. Cancellation before end or failure
      to close is source-unavailable. The host owns deadline policy and physical
      cleanup.

      started occurs exactly once as sequence 1 before every progress or terminal
      event. Its operation and conditional sessionIdentity equal the corresponding
      ExpectedCompilationRun fields; expected stages are validated through progress
      order and completed-report reconciliation. Missing, duplicate, or late started
      is protocol-invalid except that end-of-source with no frame is
      source-unavailable. For completed, report runId, ordered stages, operation, and
      conditional session identity equal the stream expectation.
    }
  }
}

expand SigilCompilationEventReader {
  constraints {
    CompilationEventReaderProtocol {
      Delegate envelope vocabulary and cross-direction ordering invariants to
      SigilCompilationEventProtocol.

      Reject duplicate event-owned JSON members before interpretation and delegate
      imported object decoding and comparison to SigilCompilationReportProtocol
      ReportWireValidation.

      For completed, every delivered stage report and diagnostic reconciles exactly
      with the terminal report, including diagnostic fingerprints and per-stage
      counts. A delivered stage-completed requires all report diagnostics for that
      stage to have been delivered and equal, because later progress proves no
      suppression occurred there.

      For each gap before a later delivered stage, every omitted report stage is
      disabled or skipped-by-dependency. If no stage interval was delivered, every
      report stage may be completed, failed, disabled, or skipped-by-dependency. If
      the last delivered stage is completed, every later report stage may have those
      four states. If the last delivered stage remains observably open, its report
      state is completed or failed, its delivered diagnostics are an equal unique
      subset of that stage's report diagnostics, and every later report stage may
      have the four terminal states. Pending, running, or cancelled is invalid in a
      completed report.

      Failed and cancelled reconcile no report and retain structural, ordering,
      stage-origin, and fingerprint-uniqueness validation for their delivered
      progress prefix.

      End-of-source after one validated terminal returns that terminal. The reader
      never reclassifies proposal, evaluator, workspace, persistence, or operation
      failures and never selects host cleanup behavior.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    CompilationEventReaderProtocol {
      Decision: Read through bounded end-of-source after terminal and let required
      cancellation cap source lifetime.

      Trade-offs: Validation waits for host closure and retains framing state.

      Discarded alternatives: Returning immediately or checking only buffered bytes
      was rejected because later chunks could contain corruption or another run.

      Consequences: Consumers agree on the exact accepted byte stream; timeout or
      closure failure is source-unavailable rather than a validated terminal.

      Revisit when: Transports provide an authenticated host-neutral run boundary.
    }

    CancellationPrecedence {
      Decision: Validate caller expectation before observing cancellation, register
      cancellation before source access, and preserve the first observed pending-read
      settlement.

      Scope: Governs input validation, listener lifetime, and cancellation races in
      validateStream; excludes the host's deadline and physical source cleanup.

      Trade-offs: Invalid caller input is reported even when already cancelled, and
      cancellation cannot erase a source result already accepted for processing.

      Discarded alternatives: Cancellation-first validation was rejected because it
      hides invalid contract input. Allowing later cancellation to replace an
      accepted chunk was rejected because scheduling could change classification of
      identical bytes.

      Consequences: Registration exists only during validation and every race has one
      deterministic winner.

      Revisit when: The host cancellation abstraction supplies atomic structured
      concurrency with equivalent precedence guarantees.
    }

    CompletedProgressReconciliation {
      Decision: Reconcile every delivered progress value exactly with the completed
      report and infer only one suppressible progress suffix.

      Scope: Governs completed-terminal validation after structurally valid serial
      progress; excludes failed and cancelled terminals, which carry no report.

      Trade-offs: Readers perform detailed comparison and reject additive or changed
      delivered evidence, while undelivered progress remains representable after one
      atomic suppression point.

      Discarded alternatives: Treating progress as unauthenticated display data was
      rejected because it could contradict the authoritative report. Requiring every
      report item to have an event was rejected because compatible progress loss must
      not fail a valid terminal result. Arbitrary omitted intervals were rejected
      because the writer suppresses a suffix, not independent events.

      Consequences: Delivered completed stages are exact; gaps before later progress
      are only disabled or dependency-skipped; the final open interval and all later
      stages follow the explicit suffix rules.

      Revisit when: A later protocol version marks each omitted range explicitly.
    }
  }

  cases {
    CompilationEventReaderProtocol {
      UTF-8 JSON split across chunks forms one frame at LF. LF and CRLF work; blank
      frames, invalid UTF-8, two JSON values, and EOF before terminal delimiter fail.

      EOF ending an incomplete UTF-8 code unit is protocol-invalid. EOF with valid
      UTF-8 but an unterminated frame or no terminal is source-unavailable. Progress,
      terminal, or a second started outside the required sequence-1 started position
      is protocol-invalid.

      Terminal followed by end returns terminal. Any trailing byte in the same or a
      later chunk is protocol-invalid. Cancellation while awaiting closure is
      source-unavailable.

      A completed report with another runId, different stages, wrong session, changed
      delivered diagnostic, or mismatched delivered stage report is protocol-invalid.
      Failed or cancelled after valid diagnostic progress is accepted without report
      reconciliation.

      Cancellation observed before a pending source settlement wins; an observed
      chunk or end is processed normally despite later cancellation. After a
      nonsettling chunk is processed, observed cancellation prevents the next read
      and returns source-unavailable; a terminal awaiting closure follows this rule.
    }
  }
}
